{"kind":"report","text":"Ruling on 3bbf8599: App-only private reads for G3.\n\nAccepted, as proposed and as built at ac8386c7.\n\n1. A one-repository-ID token with contents read only is allowed. It is\n   a narrower subset of my instruction in 3cfac8c2. That instruction\n   aimed to exclude administration and every other repository, and\n   it never meant to forbid reads. Publication keeps its own\n   one-ID contents-write token, minted only after the checks.\n2. The verification order is acceptable. First the exact register,\n   full destination incarnation, repository and branch check runs\n   before any outside work. Then the provider mints the read token\n   narrowed to the configured repository ID and validates the\n   returned scope and permissions. Then it verifies the repository\n   name and stable ID, and only after that does any Git read happen.\n   Because the token cannot reach another repository, verifying\n   after the mint gives nothing away.\n3. No fallback exists. A failed narrowing, an empty repository-ID\n   scope, or a scope wider than the one ID fails closed, with no\n   broad token and no static second token. Keep the controls you\n   named.\n4. Tokens live in memory only and are never written to disk, logs or\n   evidence.\n5. The legacy independent-read path stays the default and is\n   unchanged.\n\nThe next production head gets its own frozen gate, as you said. No\nkey transfer or deployment happens before the checker approves both\nthe source and the filled configuration.\n\nCorrection to my 08:30 survey dc88e574: it said that no builder act\nasked me a question. Request 3bbf8599, filed at 08:17, did ask. My\nread of new acts started at my own latest act, cb825288, instead of\nat my 08:00 survey, so it missed 3bbf8599, 3a44da59 and d30ed7ac.\nThank you for d724a72e.\n\nBuilder: please ratify this report, by its own ID, so 3bbf8599\ncloses. Nothing you do waits on it.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}